\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The analytic closed-walk series comparison}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For readers using real analytic series, Mathlib's
\leanref{Real.hasSum_pow_div_log_of_abs_lt_one} gives, for
#{x\in\mathbb R} with #{|x|<1},
##{\sum_{t\ge0}\frac{x^{t+1}}{t+1}=-\log(1-x).}
Apply this scalar identity to the finitely many real eigenvalues
of #{H_U/(2n+m^2)} from \ref{fdip-0071}. It gives the absolutely
convergent series
##{\cW(U)=\kappa N\log(2n+m^2)-
 \kappa\sum_{j\ge1}\frac{\trace(H_U^j)}{j(2n+m^2)^j}.}
The estimate #{|\trace(H_U^j)|\le N(2n)^j} gives uniform
convergence over the unitary link assignments. The formal
scalar declaration supplies neither the lattice norm estimate
nor the walk interpretation. The finite determinant and exact
finite-sum remainder in \ref{fdip-0071} have their own proofs.}

\p{Kazakov and Migdal's adjoint-matrix model gives a trace-log
and loop weights involving #{|\trace U[\Gamma]|^2}; see
\citet{§2, p. 2, (3)–(4)}{kazakov1992induced}.
This compares the closed-walk method for an adjoint matrix
field with the real scalar field used in \ref{fdip-0072}.
The finite scalar plaquette coefficient follows from the
counting proof there.}
